ghpull:754 a bl:PullRequest, bl:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:label "sparql_endpoint: Azure Blob Storage snapshot backend for persistence (#504)" ;
    bl:number 754 ;
    bl:state bl:Open ;
    bl:closesIssue ghissues:504 .

session:claudeSonnet5 a prov:SoftwareAgent ;
    rdfs:label "Claude Sonnet 5" .

session:pr754 a agp:AgentSession ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith session:claudeSonnet5 ;
    prov:used ghissues:504 ;
    prov:startedAtTime "2026-10-31T15:32:35Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-31T16:10:46Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

session:pr754Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #504 was filed as a follow-up split out of #58 (Auth Tier 3, incoming direction): dagalog itself acquiring and using its own Azure Managed Identity to call other Azure services, originally framed around writing persistence snapshots to Azure Blob Storage, per docs/plans/AUTH.md's pseudocode sketch (DefaultAzureCredential + BlobClient::new). Dag authorized picking this up, choosing to build the real Blob Storage backend over a simpler File Share/Disk-mount alternative (the latter needs zero code since --data-dir already works against any mounted filesystem) because a genuine Blob-backed path is useful where no shared mounted filesystem is available, e.g. multiple replicas needing one durable snapshot location. Investigated the actual persistence implementation (sparql_endpoint/src/persistence.rs, built after #504 was originally filed) before writing any code: it is built directly on redb, an embedded database requiring a real local file opened via memory-mapping, which cannot address a Blob Storage object directly -- mmap over a network filesystem is a pattern most embedded-DB authors explicitly warn against. This ruled out the issue's original 'point the database at Blob Storage' framing and led to a snapshot export/import design instead: upload the whole local redb file to Blob Storage after compaction, download it to the local path at startup if missing. This also happens to be exactly the 'Checkpoint/snapshot compaction' follow-up persistence.rs's own module doc already flagged as unimplemented, so this PR builds that compaction-to-durable-storage path for real rather than leaving it purely local. Also discovered mid-implementation that azure_identity::DefaultAzureCredential, named explicitly in the issue and AUTH.md's sketch, does not exist in the crate's current stable 1.x generation (removed before 1.0 per its own changelog) -- substituted ManagedIdentityCredential directly, which is in fact the exact credential type #504 is named for, so this is a correction to a stale design reference, not a scope change." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:754 ;
    agp:decisionPoint session:pr754Decision ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr754 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr754Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Implemented Blob Storage access as a snapshot export/import layer (upload/download the whole local redb file at compaction/startup boundaries) sitting alongside the existing local-disk persistence path, rather than trying to make redb itself address Blob Storage." ;
    agp:alternative "Pointing redb's database file directly at a Blob Storage object (e.g. via a custom mmap-compatible storage trait, or mounting Blob Storage as a filesystem via blobfuse) was rejected: redb needs real local-file semantics for its memory-mapped I/O and fsync-based durability guarantees, and mmap'ing a database file over a network filesystem is a known-risky pattern most embedded-database authors explicitly warn against -- it would trade a well-understood local-disk durability story for a fragile one, for no real benefit over an explicit snapshot upload/download step." .
